<DocsLayout title="D. Score gate" description="Reject almost every raw anchor with an exact score-only X-drop pass, then compact the rare survivors stably." wide>
  <p>The funnel is intentionally extreme. On frozen workload A, the gate preserved the exact oracle output while avoiding full HSP materialization for nearly every hit.</p>
  <pre aria-label="Measured score-gate funnel"><code>{`162,123,203  raw seed-hit anchors
     27,342  score-gate survivors  (0.0169%)
     10,680  accepted raw HSPs
      4,248  final HSPs after host dedup`}</code></pre>
  <p>Nearly every hit ends here, so each instruction the gate spends per hit counts. Each side's first X-drop comes from one ballot of the lanes with any drop, and its value is read from the first dropping lane (the maximum before that drop). The keep test is one integer comparison against a threshold the host computes once, chosen so that it equals KegAlign's `f32`-rounded comparison; the output stays byte-identical. Measured on an L4 against the earlier four-ballot recovery and `f32` keep (hg38 × mm39 unit 0, one GPU, four reversed arms per variant, output identical to the frozen digest): score gate −30.1%, GPU busy −26.1% and wall −25.3% with the default reference-bucketed kernel; score gate −25.9% and wall −23.4% with the plain kernel (two arms per variant). One work unit, device seeder on, gate kernel forced per pass; not yet measured on a whole genome. On an RTX 4090 (same unit, plain kernel): score gate −24.1%, GPU busy −21.9%, wall −18.4%.</p>
